get on your side tonight making a list  checking it twice isn't only a good idea  for Santa Claus our Savvy Shopper and  consumer savings expert has an approach  to our holiday shopping before we hit  the stores  a lot of people are worried about  affording the holidays this year with  Rising inflation and a turbulent stock  market the earlier you prepare your  finances for the holiday season the  better according to budgeting expert  Andrea warrock she says before you even  begin look at your finances figure out  how much you can afford to pay for gifts  set your budget by making a gifting  strategy list out the people who you  want to buy a gift for maybe that means  cutting back on who you give gifts to  this year because you don't have enough  money to spend speak with your family  and friends about gift expectations  instead of everyone buying a gift in  your friend group she suggests a secret  Santa gift exchange set a small budget  cap so no one feels pressure to spend  more that they can afford if you're not  good at making lists then use this free  app you can use an app like Santa's bag  it makes it really easy where you can  enter in each person's name gift idea a  budget and then the actual amount you  spent per person so at any time you can  open the app to see how much you've  spent in total there's also Christmas  gift list gift tracker and red laser  anything to help you with better buying  decisions so you don't overspend keep in  mind just because something is on sale  doesn't mean you need to buy it keeping  track of your purchases will help you  especially if you started early in  December you can look to see how much  you've spent and then decide all right  I'm done or maybe I could afford one  more thing  she adds consider making homemade gifts  you know those type of presents are  always great ways to save money while  creating something memorable consider  photos for grandparents or cookies for  your kids teachers
 

